---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PromptPage from "../../../components/PromptPage.astro";

const masterPrompt = `Act as an expert short-form video strategist, vertical-video director, editor and AI video prompt specialist.

TASK:
Create a high-retention short-form video prompt optimized for:

Facebook Reels
Instagram Reels
TikTok
YouTube Shorts

The video should be fast enough to maintain attention, but still clear, credible and visually coherent.

PLATFORM:
[Facebook Reel / Instagram Reel / TikTok / YouTube Short.]

TOPIC:
[What is the video about?]

GOAL:
[Educate / entertain / demonstrate / promote / explain / inspire / convert / other.]

TARGET AUDIENCE:
[Who is this for?]

TOTAL DURATION:
[For example: 10 sec / 15 sec / 20 sec / 30 sec / 45 sec / 60 sec.]

ASPECT RATIO:
[Default: 9:16.]

MAIN HOOK:
[What should make the viewer stop scrolling?]

CORE MESSAGE:
[What should the viewer understand or remember?]

PAYOFF:
[What does the viewer gain by watching until the end?]

CTA:
[Follow / save / comment / share / learn more / shop / none.]

SUBJECT:
[Person / food / product / process / screen / environment / other.]

VISUAL STYLE:
[Photorealistic / premium / creator-style / cinematic / educational / commercial / casual / other.]

VOICE-OVER:
[Paste script or describe tone and content.]

ON-SCREEN TEXT:
[Exact wording or none.]

MUSIC:
[Optional.]

SOUND EFFECTS:
[Optional.]

BRAND:
[Optional.]

REFERENCE REQUIREMENTS:
[Character / product / food / style details that must remain consistent.]

MANDATORY MOMENTS:
[List anything that must appear.]

ELEMENTS TO AVOID:
[Optional.]

SPECIAL REQUIREMENTS:
[Any additional instructions.]

SHORT-FORM VIDEO REQUIREMENTS:

1. OPTIMIZE FOR VERTICAL VIEWING
Default to 9:16 unless another format is specified.

2. DESIGN FOR MOBILE FIRST
Important subjects, text and actions must remain easy to understand on a phone screen.

3. KEEP CRITICAL CONTENT INSIDE SAFE AREAS
Avoid placing essential content too close to:
Top UI
Bottom UI
Left edge
Right edge

4. ESTABLISH THE SUBJECT IMMEDIATELY
The viewer should understand what the video is about within the opening moments.

5. CREATE A STRONG FIRST-SECOND HOOK
The opening should create:
Curiosity
Recognition
A useful promise
A visual surprise
A compelling result
A clear problem

6. DO NOT USE MISLEADING HOOKS
The opening must match the actual video.

7. SHOW VALUE EARLY
Do not spend several seconds on logos, introductions or unnecessary setup.

8. AVOID SLOW INTRODUCTIONS
Do not begin with:
Long establishing shots
Lengthy greetings
Logo animations
Generic cinematic filler

unless specifically requested.

9. KEEP THE OPENING VISUALLY CLEAR
Avoid clutter that makes the hook hard to understand.

10. USE ONE PRIMARY HOOK
Do not stack multiple unrelated hooks.

11. BUILD A RETENTION ARC
A useful structure is:
Hook
Progression
Value
Payoff
CTA

12. EACH MOMENT SHOULD EARN ITS SCREEN TIME
Remove shots that do not add:
Information
Emotion
Proof
Progress
Entertainment
Clarity

13. KEEP SCENES SHORT
Short-form content usually benefits from concise visual beats.

14. DO NOT CUT SO FAST THAT INFORMATION BECOMES UNREADABLE

15. MATCH CUT SPEED TO CONTENT
Educational content may require more viewing time.
Entertainment may support faster pacing.

16. VARY VISUAL SCALE
Where useful, alternate between:
Wide
Medium
Close-up
Detail

17. DO NOT CHANGE SHOT SIZE RANDOMLY
Each framing change should reveal something new.

18. CREATE PATTERN CHANGES
Use purposeful shifts in:
Shot size
Camera angle
Subject action
Visual detail
Text
Sound

to renew attention.

19. DO NOT RELY ON RANDOM EFFECTS FOR RETENTION

20. USE MOTION EARLY
When appropriate, start with visible action rather than a static setup.

21. SUBJECT MOTION SHOULD BE CLEAR
Avoid subtle actions that are difficult to notice on mobile.

22. PRIORITIZE LARGE READABLE DETAILS
Tiny objects may require close-up shots.

23. HUMAN PRESENTER MODE
Use:
Direct eye contact
Natural expression
Clear gestures
Readable body language

24. DO NOT OVER-ACT EXPRESSIONS
Keep reactions believable.

25. PRESERVE CHARACTER IDENTITY
Maintain:
Face
Hair
Age
Skin tone
Clothing
Accessories
Body proportions

26. DO NOT ALLOW FACE DRIFT BETWEEN SHOTS

27. FOOD REEL MODE
Prioritize:
Finished result
Texture
Cut
Pour
Steam
Mixing
Serving
Close-up detail

28. FOOD HOOKS SHOULD BE APPETITE-DRIVEN
Show the finished dish or most visually satisfying moment early when appropriate.

29. PRESERVE FOOD CONTINUITY
Do not change:
Dish shape
Layers
Colour
Toppings
Serving vessel

without reason.

30. PRODUCT REEL MODE
Show the product clearly in the opening or very early.

31. PRESERVE PRODUCT IDENTITY
Maintain:
Shape
Colour
Logo
Packaging
Features
Materials

32. DO NOT INVENT PRODUCT FEATURES

33. PROCESS / TUTORIAL MODE
Prioritize meaningful steps.

34. DO NOT SHOW EVERY MICRO-STEP
Only show what helps understanding.

35. USE STEP COMPRESSION
Combine repetitive actions when possible.

36. BEFORE / AFTER MODE
Only use when both states are truthful and supported.

37. DO NOT FABRICATE RESULTS

38. EDUCATIONAL MODE
Use:
Question
Explanation
Example
Takeaway

39. TIP VIDEO MODE
Lead with the problem or practical result.

40. LIST MODE
Keep item count manageable for the duration.

41. STORY MODE
Use:
Immediate situation
Progress
Change
Resolution

42. COMMERCIAL MODE
Show:
Problem or desire
Product
Use
Benefit
CTA

43. KEEP THE CTA SHORT
Do not turn the ending into a long sales pitch.

44. MATCH CTA TO THE GOAL
Examples:
Save this
Follow for more
Try it
Learn more
Shop now
Comment below

45. DO NOT USE FALSE URGENCY

46. DO NOT USE FALSE SCARCITY

47. DO NOT CLAIM GUARANTEED RESULTS

48. DO NOT INVENT SOCIAL PROOF
No fake:
Views
Likes
Reviews
Ratings
Testimonials
Follower counts

49. DEFINE SCENE DURATION
Every scene should have a realistic time allocation.

50. DO NOT OVERLOAD A 2-SECOND SHOT
Avoid placing:
Long narration
Multiple actions
Large text
Complex camera movement

inside a very short moment.

51. TIME VOICE-OVER REALISTICALLY
Spoken content must fit naturally within the duration.

52. SIMPLIFY WORDING FOR SHORT-FORM
Prefer conversational language and concise sentences.

53. ALIGN VOICE-OVER WITH VISUAL ACTION

54. DO NOT SHOW SOMETHING UNRELATED TO THE NARRATION

55. USE VISUAL PROOF WHERE POSSIBLE
If explaining an action, show it.

56. ALLOW SILENT MOMENTS
Not every second needs narration.

57. USE SOUND EFFECTS TO EMPHASIZE REAL ACTION
Examples:
Cut
Pour
Click
Crunch
Sizzle
Door close
Packaging open

58. DO NOT OVERLOAD SOUND EFFECTS

59. MUSIC SHOULD SUPPORT PACING
Not compete with voice-over.

60. ON-SCREEN TEXT SHOULD BE SHORT
Keep text readable at mobile size.

61. USE HIGH TEXT CONTRAST

62. LIMIT TEXT PER SCREEN

63. KEEP IMPORTANT TEXT LONG ENOUGH TO READ

64. DO NOT GENERATE RANDOM TEXT

65. USE EXACT PROVIDED WORDING

66. RECOMMEND MANUAL TEXT OVERLAY WHEN ACCURACY IS CRITICAL

67. CAPTION SAFE-AREA MODE
Leave space where platform captions may appear.

68. PRESERVE SUBJECT VISIBILITY BEHIND TEXT

69. KEEP CTA AWAY FROM PLATFORM UI

70. DEFINE FIRST FRAME
The opening image should make sense even before motion begins.

71. DEFINE FINAL FRAME
The ending should provide:
Payoff
Result
Hero image
CTA
Resolution

72. LOOP MODE
When useful, design the ending so it can transition naturally back into the beginning.

73. DO NOT FORCE A LOOP WHEN IT DAMAGES THE STORY

74. CAMERA MOVEMENT SHOULD BE SIMPLE
Use one dominant move per short scene.

75. USE STATIC CAMERA WHEN SUBJECT ACTION IS STRONG ENOUGH

76. USE PUSH-IN FOR EMPHASIS

77. USE TRACKING FOR ACTION

78. USE CLOSE-UPS FOR DETAIL

79. USE OVERHEAD FOR PROCESS CLARITY

80. AVOID EXCESSIVE ORBITS

81. DO NOT ADD RANDOM WHIP PANS

82. TRANSITIONS SHOULD SUPPORT PACING
Default to clean cuts.

83. USE MATCH CUTS WHEN THEY IMPROVE FLOW

84. USE SPEED RAMPS ONLY WHEN APPROPRIATE

85. DO NOT USE TRANSITIONS AS FILLER

86. PRESERVE SCREEN DIRECTION

87. PRESERVE OBJECT STATE
If something changes during the video, later shots should reflect it.

88. PRESERVE PROP CONTINUITY

89. PRESERVE ENVIRONMENT CONTINUITY
When scenes occur in the same location.

90. KEEP LIGHTING STYLE COHERENT

91. CREATE A VISUAL PRIORITY FOR EVERY SHOT
The viewer should know where to look.

92. AVOID CLUTTERED BACKGROUNDS

93. KEEP MOBILE-SIZE SUBJECT SEPARATION STRONG

94. CREATE TARGETED NEGATIVE CONSTRAINTS
Examples:
No random text
No face drift
No duplicate hands
No product redesign
No object teleportation
No warped food
No background morphing

95. CREATE A RETENTION CHECK
Ask whether each scene gives the viewer a reason to continue.

96. CREATE A MOBILE READABILITY CHECK
Review the composition at small-screen scale.

97. CREATE A CLAIM CHECK
Verify any:
Numbers
Benefits
Results
Offers
Prices

before publishing.

98. CREATE A PLATFORM CHECK
Make sure the video structure fits the selected short-form platform.

99. CREATE AN AUDIO TIMING CHECK
Ensure voice-over, music and sounds fit the final duration.

100. FINAL QUALITY CHECK
Before finalizing verify:

- platform is clear
- aspect ratio is correct
- first frame is strong
- hook appears immediately
- hook accurately represents the video
- subject is mobile-readable
- safe zones are respected
- pacing matches the content
- scenes are not overloaded
- every scene adds value
- visual variety has a purpose
- character identity is consistent
- product identity is consistent
- food identity is consistent
- object states remain logical
- voice-over fits the runtime
- narration matches the visuals
- text is concise
- text remains readable
- no random text is generated
- CTA is brief
- CTA matches the video goal
- no fake urgency is used
- no fake scarcity is used
- no unsupported result is shown
- final payoff is clear
- the video feels complete even at short duration

OUTPUT FORMAT:

1. SHORT-FORM STRATEGY
Summarize:
Platform
Audience
Hook
Core message
Payoff
CTA

2. RETENTION STRUCTURE
Provide:
Opening hook
Development
Pattern changes
Payoff
Ending

3. TIMELINE

For each scene provide:
Scene number
Timestamp
Duration
Purpose
Subject
Action
Shot size
Camera movement
Voice-over
Visible text
Sound
Transition

4. FIRST-FRAME DESIGN
Describe the exact opening composition.

5. FULL SHORT-FORM VIDEO PROMPT
Provide a polished model-ready prompt.

6. VOICE-OVER TIMING
Map each spoken line to the appropriate visual moment.

7. TEXT & SAFE-ZONE PLAN
Specify:
Exact text
Placement
Reading time
Safe-area considerations

8. AUDIO PLAN
Specify:
Music
Ambient sound
Sound effects

9. NEGATIVE / PROTECTION INSTRUCTIONS
List the most relevant short-form generation failures to prevent.

10. FINAL FRAME / CTA
Describe the final visual and CTA treatment.

11. MOBILE RETENTION CHECK
Confirm:
Hook clarity
Small-screen readability
Pacing
Payoff
CTA

IMPORTANT:
- Optimize for mobile-first vertical viewing.
- Deliver value immediately.
- Keep the first-second hook truthful.
- Remove unnecessary filler.
- Match action, narration and text to the available duration.
- Use purposeful visual changes rather than random effects.
- Protect character, food and product continuity.
- Keep CTA short.
- Do not fabricate results, social proof, urgency or scarcity.`;

const steps = [
  {
    title: "Start with the scroll-stopping moment",
    description:
      "Choose the strongest truthful visual, question, result or action for the opening instead of wasting the first seconds on introductions.",
  },
  {
    title: "Build a retention timeline",
    description:
      "Break the total runtime into short visual beats where each scene adds new information, progress, proof or payoff.",
  },
  {
    title: "Design for a phone screen",
    description:
      "Keep the subject large, protect caption and interface safe areas, and limit on-screen wording so everything remains readable vertically.",
  },
  {
    title: "End with payoff before CTA",
    description:
      "Give viewers the result or useful takeaway they were promised before asking them to follow, save, comment, learn more or take another action.",
  },
];

const example = {
  input: [
    "Platform: Facebook Reel.",
    "Duration: 20 seconds.",
    "Aspect ratio: 9:16.",
    "Topic: Homemade chocolate brownies.",
    "Audience: Home bakers.",
    "Goal: Show the result and summarize the preparation.",
    "Hook: Break open a brownie to reveal the moist fudgy centre.",
    "CTA: Save this recipe.",
  ],

  output: [
    "0–2 SEC — HOOK: Extreme close-up of one brownie being gently broken apart to reveal the moist fudgy centre. Keep the brownie large in frame and immediately readable on mobile.",
    "2–6 SEC — MIXTURE: Show melted chocolate mixture followed by a clean close-up of the batter being folded. Use short purposeful cuts.",
    "6–10 SEC — PAN: Show batter spreading into the prepared baking pan, then a close detail of chocolate pieces on top.",
    "10–14 SEC — RESULT TRANSITION: Move directly from the baked tray to neat brownie squares without unnecessary oven filler.",
    "14–18 SEC — PAYOFF: Return to the strongest fudgy interior close-up, using a gentle push-in.",
    "18–20 SEC — CTA: Finished brownie hero shot with the short text 'Save this recipe'. Keep the text inside the central mobile safe area.",
  ],
};

const tips = [
  {
    title: "The hook should reveal, not deceive",
    description:
      "Strong retention does not require clickbait. Show the most interesting real moment early and then deliver the information or result it promises.",
  },
  {
    title: "Shorter is not always faster",
    description:
      "A close-up texture reveal may need two or three seconds to register. Fast cutting only helps when viewers still have enough time to understand each shot.",
  },
  {
    title: "Check the video at phone size",
    description:
      "A composition that looks clear on a desktop can become unreadable in a vertical feed. Review subject size, text, captions and CTA placement at realistic mobile scale.",
  },
];

---

<BaseLayout
  title="Short-Form Reel Video Prompt | KRIYANO"
  description="Create high-retention AI video prompts for Facebook Reels, Instagram Reels, TikTok and YouTube Shorts with better hooks, pacing, safe zones and mobile-first storytelling."
>
  <PromptPage
    category="VIDEO GENERATION"
    title="Short-Form Reel Video"
    accentTitle="Prompt."
    description="Create mobile-first AI video prompts for Reels, TikTok and Shorts with stronger opening hooks, pacing, visual retention, voice-over timing and payoff."
    meta={[
      "Free prompt",
      "Reels & Shorts",
      "9:16 video",
    ]}
    bestFor={[
      "Facebook Reels",
      "Instagram Reels",
      "TikTok",
      "YouTube Shorts",
      "Recipe videos",
      "Creator content",
    ]}
    masterPrompt={masterPrompt}
    introTitle="Win the first second, then keep delivering."
    introDescription="Start with a truthful visual hook, keep every scene useful, control pacing for mobile viewing and deliver the promised payoff before the final CTA."
    steps={steps}
    exampleTitle="Turn a brownie recipe into a concise vertical Reel."
    example={example}
    tipsTitle="Retention comes from useful progression, not random effects."
    tips={tips}
    sidebarTitle="Need a longer multi-scene production?"
    sidebarDescription="Use KRIYANO's Multi-Scene Video Prompt when you need more detailed continuity, story structure, scene planning and editing across a longer sequence."
    warningTitle="Fast pacing can hide generation mistakes"
    warningText="Review character identity, food and product continuity, text accuracy, object states and claims frame by frame before publishing. Short-form speed should not be used to conceal inaccurate AI-generated details."
  />
</BaseLayout>